{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nA Jira update currently crosses `dispatch_one._update_one_scalar_update`, generic `dispatch_apply_phases._call_with_retry`, Cloud ACLI retry, or Jira DC SDK and `_with_connection_retry` loops. A timeout can multiply calls or leave an accepted write ambiguous. Jira updates expose no Rebar-controlled idempotency key, so recovery needs desired-state observation instead of blind replay.\n\n## Outcome\nProve the provider-neutral contract by setting an issue summary to an exact value. Cloud follows `dispatch_one._update_one_scalar_update -> AcliClient.update_issue(key, summary=...)`; DC terminates at `_IssuesMixin.update_issue(key, summary=...)`. Both return typed outcomes from one logical-operation budget. This story establishes ADR 0100; RP-03 S5 finalizes it after all families migrate.\n\n## Approach\n1. Define operation/outcome values with stable logical id, desired postcondition, attempt count, disposition, failure scope, replay safety, provider delay, `retry_not_before`, plus bounded redacted diagnostics.\n2. A physical invocation is one executor call to an adapter write or observation, not each required request inside it. Count raw provider requests separately. The shared owner permits three physical invocations and 15 seconds cumulative sleep across initial write, observation, plus safe replan. Use `2**(attempt+1) + U[0,1)` jitter: [2s,3s) then [4s,5s). Reuse `_errors.parse_retry_after`; provider delay is a lower bound.\n3. Route only an exact `{\"summary\"}` field set to the executor. Mixed `{\"summary\",\"description\"}` stays one unsplit legacy call in S1. The flag defaults to the executor when this slice lands. Legacy is an explicit pre-call rollback value; no dual-send. A poison test proves generic retry is unreachable.\n4. Cloud retains one process and shared cleanup: POSIX group reap or non-POSIX child kill/bounded wait, with partial output. Rebar adds no replay below the budget and claims no control over undocumented vendor-binary retry. Decode exit/stdout/stderr only from sanitized historical shapes, including captured exit-1 unauthorized output plus ticket timeout evidence. Unknown shapes fail loud as unclassified; REST observations use structured status/header decoding.\n5. DC `build_client_from_settings` passes `jira.JIRA(..., max_retries=0)`; a fail-fast guard checks the resulting session. The migrated summary entrypoint invokes each pre-read/write/read-back through `_with_connection_retry` with a single-attempt, no-sleep policy. Legacy routes keep today's wrapper until cutover.\n6. One DC summary-write invocation has fixed raw requests GET issue -> PUT fields -> GET read-back, with no SDK/wrapper repeats. Failure ends that invocation. Successful read-back is its authoritative result; after ambiguous PUT, a separate GET observation consumes the next physical invocation. Tests assert top-level attempts plus raw order.\n7. After ambiguous timeout, desired summary observed means recovered success. A conclusive old value may replay inside remaining budget. Failed, rate-limited, or inconclusive observation returns non-replaying `commit_unknown` with deferral metadata; no fallthrough write.\n8. Worked example: DC invocation 1 issues GET then ambiguous PUT; observation 2 issues one GET that times out. Return `commit_unknown` after two invocations, three requests, plus one PUT. If observation 2 returns the old summary, invocation 3 may run GET/PUT/GET; no fourth invocation. A poison sleeper proves `_with_connection_retry` never delays or replays.\n9. Credential, TLS, permission, invalid-request, plus other permanent failures do not retry. Eligible network failures, 429, plus retryable 5xx use the shared schedule. Over-budget authoritative delay returns `retry_not_before` without early sleep or fictional attempt.\n10. Emit one bounded redacted operation-completion log for `commit_unknown`, `retry_not_before`, exhaustion, plus child cleanup failure. Tests pin fields, level, redaction, and size.\n11. Establish ADR 0100 with the contract, retry owner, Jira idempotency limitation, observe-before-replay rationale, budget, deltas, plus rollback. Relate ADR 0084/0087 and desired-state ADRs 0004/0026/0029: observation reuses existing convergence; the new decision is one budget across dispatch, CLI, SDK, and adapter loops. The 15-second cumulative budget supersedes ADR 0084's 60-second per-sleep cap for migrated routes; longer delays become `retry_not_before`.\n\n## Scope\n- `operation_outcome.py`, `retry_budget.py`: contract and sole budget.\n- `dispatch_one.py`, `dispatch_apply_phases.py`: default-on route and wrapper bypass.\n- Cloud adapters: one-process execution, observation, decoding, cleanup, typed surface.\n- DC `transport.py`, `retry.py`, `_issues.py`, backend: SDK disabled, one-attempt wrapper policy, fixed request shape.\n- ADR 0100, budget/outcome tests, historical ACLI fixtures, reaping regressions, DC request tests.\n\n## Testing\n- Fake-clock tables assert attempts, requests, sleep, deadlines, provider lower bounds, plus `retry_not_before` with no real sleep.\n- Stateful Cloud/DC fakes cover success, disconnect, ambiguity, inconclusive observation, recovery, 429/503, malformed delay, TLS/auth/permanent 4xx, plus request order.\n- Poison tests fail on generic retry, DC wrapper replay/sleep, SDK retry, or an extra ACLI process.\n- Historical ACLI triples pin decoding; unknown shapes fail loud. Real child-process tests prove reaping.\n- Output tests pin logs, redaction, bounded diagnostics, plus fast-fail behavior. Required tests are local and credential-free; S5 `diamond-flavoured-esok` owns venue-gated live confirmation before contract removal.\n\n## Compatibility / Rollback\nThe summary flag ships default-on for the executor; legacy is the rollback value. Rollback preserves successful effects, then re-observes ambiguity. Intentional deltas are the shared three-invocation/15-second budget, long-delay deferral, fail-loud unknown ACLI shapes, plus non-replaying inconclusive recovery.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Both summary routes return the same provider-neutral outcome schema; deterministic tests assert every field.\n- [ ] Repeated physical invocations preserve one logical id; a unit test asserts identity stability.\n- [ ] Top-level counters prove no postcondition exceeds three invocations or 15 seconds cumulative sleep.\n- [ ] Raw counters prove each DC write invocation follows GET/PUT/GET without hidden repeats.\n- [ ] Exact `{\"summary\"}` bypasses `_call_with_retry`; a poison test proves it.\\n- [ ] Mixed fields stay one legacy call; a table test proves no split.\n- [ ] DC construction passes `max_retries=0`; a guard test rejects a nonzero session value.\n- [ ] Migrated DC calls use single-attempt/no-sleep `_with_connection_retry`; poison tests reject replay or sleep.\n- [ ] Fallback sleeps stay in [2s,3s), then [4s,5s); fake-clock tests pin bounds.\\n- [ ] Over-budget provider delay returns `retry_not_before` without sleeping.\n- [ ] Permanent failures perform one invocation; transient failures follow the injected schedule.\n- [ ] Satisfied post-timeout observation returns recovered success without a second write.\n- [ ] Conclusively unsatisfied observation permits only remaining-budget replay.\n- [ ] Failed or inconclusive observation returns non-replaying `commit_unknown`; the example asserts two invocations, three requests, one PUT.\n- [ ] ACLI cleanup tests pass for POSIX groups and non-POSIX direct children.\n- [ ] Historical ACLI triples decode as expected; an unknown triple returns unclassified without retry.\n- [ ] Completion logs expose deferred/exhausted outcomes without secret or unbounded content.\n- [ ] ADR 0100 records the contract and prior-art relationships; index checks pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787025872124065001,"uuid":"9e7ccb57-5362-4a64-bfb8-b7b7c761b343"}